 The clock pane's face and map are PAINTED, not merely reserved.

 desk-clock-art draws the dial and the coastline over the laid tree, and it
 had NO CALLERS. desk-wnd-over is the door it comes through and that function
 answered 0 for every focus id except the launcher's, so neither picture had
 ever reached the glass. The prose above desk-clock-art described the wiring
 in detail and the wiring was absent, which is why a comment is not evidence
 that a path runs (L-UNCALLED).

 NOTHING ALREADY IN THE TREE COULD SEE IT. clock-face and clock-world drive
 face-draw and wld-draw directly, so both stayed green while the pane they
 exist for painted nothing; the pane's own wiring was never armed. A test of
 a drawing primitive is not a test that anything calls it.

 THE ARM PAINTS INTO A BUFFER AND COUNTS. face-draw and wld-draw take a base
 pointer and a stride and are otherwise pure, so a plain allocation stands in
 for the framebuffer and no guest GOP is needed. Stride is in PIXELS and also
 clips the span (gop-fill-rect compares px + pw against it), so the buffer is
 stride * height * 4 bytes and nothing can write outside it.

 The control is the same call under another pane's focus id, which must light
 nothing. Without it a row reading "some pixels are set" would pass on a
 painter that ignored the id and drew for everybody.

 WHAT THIS ARM CANNOT SEE, stated because the first version of it was quoted
 as though it could. It BUILDS the world blob itself, so it proves the painter
 draws a coastline it is handed and says nothing about whether the pane ever
 fills that cell. It did not: `clk-ensure2` had no callers and the map came up
 as bare grid stripes on a real desk while every row here was green. The blob
 row below is the narrowest honest version of the question, a paint with the
 cell as production left it against a paint with it filled, and the wiring
 itself is answered by `desk-clock-open` calling `clk-ensure2` and by nothing
 in this file.

Section: Test

  art-w : Integer = 1024
  art-h : Integer = 768

  lit-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lit-row (buf) (stride) (x) (y) (x1) (acc) =
    if x >= x1 then acc
    else
      let v = peek-32 buf ((y * stride + x) * 4)
      in lit-row buf stride (x + 1) y x1 (if v /= 0 then acc + 1 else acc)

  lit-rect : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  lit-rect (buf) (stride) (b) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= b.lr-y + b.lr-h then acc
    else if y < 0 then lit-rect buf stride b (y + 1) acc
    else if y >= art-h then acc
    else lit-rect buf stride b (y + 1) (acc + lit-row buf stride (b.lr-x) y (b.lr-x + b.lr-w) 0)

 A COUNT OF LIT PIXELS CANNOT SEE A HAND MOVE. The dial's disc is filled and
 the map's sea is filled, so a hand swinging from three to nine, or land
 appearing over water, changes the COLOUR of pixels that were already non-zero
 and leaves the count identical. The first version of the two rows below
 counted, both read the same number for arms that differ, and both were red
 for that reason and not because the pane was broken. Summing the pixel VALUES
 is an oracle whose output shape can express the distinction.

  sum-row : Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-row (buf) (stride) (x) (y) (x1) (acc) =
    if x >= x1 then acc
    else sum-row buf stride (x + 1) y x1 (acc + peek-32 buf ((y * stride + x) * 4))

  sum-rect : Integer, Integer, LayoutRect, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sum-rect (buf) (stride) (b) (y) (acc) =
    if y >= b.lr-y + b.lr-h then acc
    else if y < 0 then sum-rect buf stride b (y + 1) acc
    else if y >= art-h then acc
    else sum-rect buf stride b (y + 1) (acc + sum-row buf stride (b.lr-x) y (b.lr-x + b.lr-w) 0)

  fresh-ds : Integer -> Integer
  fresh-ds (ks) =
    let ds = alloc-zeroed dk-ds-bytes 64
    in let a = poke-32 ds dk-adorn-cell dk-adorn-default
    in let g = poke-32 ds desk-gpu-cell 1
    in let c = poke-32 ds desk-clock-cell ks
    in ds

 One paint of one focus id into its own buffer, answering the lit count inside
 the box the tree reserved for the picture.

  paint : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Integer
  paint (ds) (ks) (fid) (boxid) =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed (art-w * art-h * 4) 64
    in let laid = desk-clock-laid art-w art-h gfont-none ds ks
    in let ov = desk-wnd-over buf art-w art-h (ui-wscale art-w) gfont-none fid laid ds
    in let b = desk-clock-box laid boxid (ui-wscale art-w)
    in if b.lr-w <= 0 then 0 - 1
    else lit-rect buf art-w b (b.lr-y) 0

 THE RIGHT HALF OF THE DIAL IS WHERE THREE O'CLOCK IS AND NINE IS NOT, so two
 paints an hour apart in opposite directions must not light it equally. A bare
 count over the whole box cannot say this: the dial and its ticks dominate the
 count and the hands are symmetric about the centre, so the totals can agree
 while the hands stand still.

  paint-at : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  paint-at (ds) (ks) (hour) =
    let n = clk-note-now ks hour 0 0 1 1 26
    in let buf = alloc-zeroed (art-w * art-h * 4) 64
    in let laid = desk-clock-laid art-w art-h gfont-none ds ks
    in let ov = desk-wnd-over buf art-w art-h (ui-wscale art-w) gfont-none desk-focus-clkp laid ds
    in let b = desk-clock-box laid "clk-art" (ui-wscale art-w)
    in let half = layout-rect (b.lr-x + b.lr-w / 2) (b.lr-y) (b.lr-w / 2) (b.lr-h)
    in sum-rect buf art-w half (half.lr-y) 0

  paint-sum : Integer, Integer, Integer, Text -> Integer
  paint-sum (ds) (ks) (fid) (boxid) =
    let buf = alloc-zeroed (art-w * art-h * 4) 64
    in let laid = desk-clock-laid art-w art-h gfont-none ds ks
    in let ov = desk-wnd-over buf art-w art-h (ui-wscale art-w) gfont-none fid laid ds
    in let b = desk-clock-box laid boxid (ui-wscale art-w)
    in if b.lr-w <= 0 then 0 - 1
    else sum-rect buf art-w b (b.lr-y) 0

  yn : Boolean -> Text
  yn (v) = if v then "yes" else "no"

 THE TWO ROWS ABOVE ARM THE PAINTER, THESE TWO ARM THE WIRING, and the
 difference is the whole reason this section exists. `paint-at` writes the
 cells itself, so it asks whether the dial follows the block and would stay
 green on a desk that never wrote the block at all, which is exactly the desk
 that shipped. `desk-wnd-tree` is the function that reads the part on every
 repaint, so calling it and looking at the cells afterwards is the question a
 person asks by watching the second hand.

 THE DAY IS THE CELL TO TEST AND THE HOUR IS NOT. A day register reads 1 to 31
 and is never 0, so a populated block is distinguishable from a zeroed one at
 any time of day; an hour is legitimately 0 for an hour every night and a row
 keyed on it would fail once a day for a correct desk.

  note-probe : Integer, Boolean -> [Device.Port] Integer
  note-probe (ks) (editing) = act
    let ds = fresh-ds ks
    in let buf = alloc-zeroed (art-w * art-h * 4) 64
    in let e = poke-32 ks clk-editing-cell (if editing then 1 else 0)
    in let z = poke-32 ks clk-d-cell 0
    in act
      t <- desk-wnd-tree desk-focus-clkp ds buf art-w art-h art-w gfont-none
      peek-32 ks clk-d-cell
    end
  end

  opening : [Console, Device.Port] Nothing =
    let ks = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let ds = fresh-ds ks
    in let f = paint ds ks desk-focus-clkp "clk-art"
    in let ctl = paint ds ks desk-focus-mon "clk-art"
    in let ks2 = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let ds2 = fresh-ds ks2
    in let w0 = poke-32 ks2 clk-world-cell wld-build
    in let v0 = clk-set-view ks2 clk-view-zones
    in let m = paint ds2 ks2 desk-focus-clkp "clk-map"
    in let ks3 = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let ds3 = fresh-ds ks3
    in let v3 = clk-set-view ks3 clk-view-zones
    in let m0 = paint-sum ds3 ks3 desk-focus-clkp "clk-map"
    in let ms = paint-sum ds2 ks2 desk-focus-clkp "clk-map"
    in let ks4 = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let ds4 = fresh-ds ks4
    in let at3 = paint-at ds4 ks4 3
    in let at9 = paint-at ds4 ks4 9
    in let ks5 = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in let ks6 = alloc-zeroed 128 64
    in act
      noted <- note-probe ks5 False
      held <- note-probe ks6 True
      print-line-uni ("face box found: " & yn (f /= 0 - 1))
      print-line-uni ("face paints: " & yn (f > 100))
      print-line-uni ("map box found: " & yn (m /= 0 - 1))
      print-line-uni ("map paints: " & yn (m > 100))
      print-line-uni ("another pane paints nothing: " & yn (ctl == 0))
      print-line-uni ("map needs the blob: " & yn (ms /= m0))
      print-line-uni ("hands follow the clock: " & yn (at3 /= at9))
      print-line-uni ("the tree arm notes the time: " & yn (noted /= 0))
      print-line-uni ("and holds off while editing: " & yn (held == 0))
    end
